---
// Goal: show that ThinkThen and its bindings add a few milliseconds to a
// call, and that Jev's reply takes nearly all of the time.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { BINDINGS } from '../../data/catalog.mjs';
import data from '../../data/overhead.json';

const ms = (x, places = 1) => (x == null ? 'none' : x.toFixed(places));
const median = (xs) => {
  const s = [...xs].sort((a, b) => a - b);
  const m = Math.floor(s.length / 2);
  return s.length % 2 ? s[m] : (s[m - 1] + s[m]) / 2;
};
const PER_ROW = new Set(['sqlite filter', 'postgresql filter']);
const singleLive = (s) => Object.entries(s.cells)
  .filter(([fn, c]) => fn !== 'recognize' && !PER_ROW.has(`${s.id} ${fn}`) && c.live_each_ms)
  .flatMap(([, c]) => c.live_each_ms);
const route = (id) => {
  if (id.startsWith('cli')) return '/install/shell/';
  const slug = id === 'c-release' ? 'c' : id;
  return BINDINGS.find((b) => b.slug === slug)?.route;
};
const byId = Object.fromEntries(data.surfaces.map((s) => [s.id, s]));
const core = byId.rust;
const coreSingle = data.cases.filter((fn) => fn !== 'recognize').map((fn) => core.cells[fn].loop_ms);
const libraries = data.surfaces.filter((s) => !s.id.startsWith('cli') && s.id !== 'rust')
  .sort((a, b) => a.added_median_ms - b.added_median_ms);
const cliLive = median(singleLive(byId.cli));
const libLive = data.head.live_median_ms;
const h = data.head;
const t = data.batches;
const tokens = { input: 285800, output: 57300 };
---
<Base title="Overhead" description="ThinkThen spends about 2 ms of a call on its own work. Jev spends about 138 ms. A binding adds under 3 ms more.">
  <h1>Overhead</h1>
  <p class="lede">A call to Jev took a median of {ms(h.live_median_ms)} ms on 2026-10-01. ThinkThen's own work took about 2 ms of it. A binding adds under 3 ms more.</p>

  <h2>The answer</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Part of a call</th><th>Median (ms)</th></tr>
    <tr><td>A whole live call through a binding, {h.live_n} calls</td><td>{ms(h.live_median_ms)}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The HTTP request inside a live call, {h.http_n} calls that report it</td><td>{ms(h.http_median_ms)}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A live call less its HTTP request, the same {h.http_n} calls</td><td>{ms(h.gap_median_ms)}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The Rust core against a local server, one request</td><td>{ms(Math.min(...coreSingle), 2)} to {ms(Math.max(...coreSingle), 2)}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A binding over the Rust core, the slowest binding</td><td>{ms(libraries.at(-1).added_median_ms, 2)}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The command over the Rust core, per run, debug build</td><td>{ms(byId.cli.added_median_ms, 2)}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>The command over the Rust core, per run, release build</td><td>{ms(byId['cli-release'].added_median_ms, 2)}</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>The HTTP request holds Jev's work and the network together. Jev sent no server time with any reply in this run. So this page cannot split Jev's time from the network's time.</p>

  <h2>How it was measured</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The builds came from checkpoint 3, published on 2026-10-01 from commit <code>bfc180a10</code>. The published command and the published C library are debug builds. A release build of each from the same commit gives the comparison.</li>
    <li>The machine is an AMD Ryzen 7 5825U with 8 cores, 16 threads and 27 GB of memory, on Linux for x86_64. Other jobs shared it. The load average was {data.load[0]} when the paid run started and {data.load[1]} when it ended.</li>
    <li>Each binding asked the same ten questions. Each question starts from the site's own sample for that function and keeps its input small. The answer cache was off for every timed call.</li>
    <li>Each binding timed its own calls with its language's clock. The clock wraps the call alone. The command was timed as a whole process.</li>
    <li>A test server on the same machine answers each request at once. Against it, each binding made one warm-up call and 20 timed calls for each function, three times over. The tables give the median of those 60 calls and the median of each set of 20.</li>
    <li>The paid run made one warm-up call for each binding. Then it made two timed calls to Jev for each function. It ran from 13:56 to 13:58 UTC.</li>
    <li>The Rust binding, built for release, stands for the core. "Added" is a binding's median minus the core's median for the same function. A number below zero means the two differ by less than the noise.</li>
    <li>The C, C++, Objective-C, COBOL, Ada, Go, Zig, Swift, PHP, Java, Kotlin, Scala, C#, Dart and Rust bindings report the time of each HTTP request. The other bindings give no HTTP column.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The spend</h2>
  <p>A counting run against the test server came first. It counted {data.plan.requests} requests, {data.plan.questions.toLocaleString('en-US')} questions and {data.plan.bytes.toLocaleString('en-US')} request bytes. The paid run sent the same requests. It held a reservation of 2,000,000 tokens under a cap of one dollar. The replies reported about {tokens.input.toLocaleString('en-US')} input tokens and {tokens.output.toLocaleString('en-US')} output tokens. Seven bindings report no token counts, and their share is counted as equal to the rest. The run cost about 1.4 cents.</p>

  <h2>Each binding</h2>
  <p>A binding adds its median over the core across nine functions. The <code>recognize</code> function sends two requests one after the other. It is left out of the medians, and the tables below show it. The live column gives the median of the binding's paid calls that send one request.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Binding</th><th>Added, median (ms)</th><th>Added, largest (ms)</th><th>Live, median (ms)</th></tr>
    <tr><td><a href={route('rust')}>{core.label}</a></td><td>{ms(0, 2)}</td><td>{ms(0, 2)}</td><td>{ms(median(singleLive(core)))}</td></tr>
    {libraries.map((s) => (
      <tr><td><a href={route(s.id)}>{s.label}</a></td><td>{ms(s.added_median_ms, 2)}</td><td>{ms(s.added_max_ms, 2)}</td><td>{s.id === 'c-release' ? 'none' : ms(median(singleLive(s)))}</td></tr>
    ))}
  </table>
  <p>The debug C library accounts for most of the time the C-interface bindings add. The release C library adds nothing past the noise. pandas and Polars lack <code>filter</code>, <code>rank</code>, <code>find</code> and <code>relate</code>. SQLite and PostgreSQL send one request for each row in <code>filter</code>. Their <code>filter</code> calls send two requests here.</p>

  <h2>The command</h2>
  <p>Each run of the command starts a process, reads its settings and writes its usage totals to disk. Each run also opens a new connection to Jev. A library keeps its connection open between calls. So a live command run took a median of {ms(cliLive)} ms against {ms(libLive)} ms through a library.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Build</th><th>Per run, test server (ms)</th><th>Over the core (ms)</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Debug, as published</td><td>{ms(median(data.cases.filter((f) => f !== 'recognize').map((f) => byId.cli.cells[f].loop_ms)), 2)}</td><td>{ms(byId.cli.added_median_ms, 2)}</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Release, same commit</td><td>{ms(median(data.cases.filter((f) => f !== 'recognize').map((f) => byId['cli-release'].cells[f].loop_ms)), 2)}</td><td>{ms(byId['cli-release'].added_median_ms, 2)}</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h2>Large inputs</h2>
  <p>Two runs of the command sent many records at once. One ranked 306 song titles. The other decided over 1,000 order messages. Each ran once live and three times against the test server.</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Input</th><th>Requests</th><th>Live (ms)</th><th>HTTP (ms)</th><th>Test server, debug (ms)</th><th>Test server, release (ms)</th></tr>
    {[['rank-306', '306 song titles'], ['lines-1000', '1,000 messages']].map(([k, name]) => (
      <tr><td>{name}</td><td>{t[k].live_facts.requests_sent}</td><td>{ms(t[k].live_ms)}</td><td>{t[k].live_http_ms.join(' and ')}</td><td>{t[k].loopback.cli.each_ms.map((x) => ms(x)).join(', ')}</td><td>{t[k].loopback['cli-release'].each_ms.map((x) => ms(x)).join(', ')}</td></tr>
    ))}
  </table>
  <p>The two requests for 1,000 messages ran side by side. On a large input the debug build takes about three times as long as the release build. The release build stays under 100 ms.</p>

  <h2>Every call</h2>
  <p>Each table gives one function. "Test server" is the median of 60 calls. "Sets" gives the median of each set of 20. "Live" gives both paid calls.</p>
  {data.cases.map((fn) => (
    <>
      <h3><a href={`/functions/${fn}/`}><code>{fn}</code></a></h3>
      <table>
        <tr><th>Binding</th><th>Test server (ms)</th><th>Sets (ms)</th><th>Added (ms)</th><th>Live (ms)</th><th>HTTP (ms)</th></tr>
        {data.surfaces.filter((s) => s.cells[fn]).map((s) => {
          const c = s.cells[fn];
          return <tr><td>{s.label}</td><td>{ms(c.loop_ms, 2)}</td><td>{c.loop_pass_ms.map((x) => ms(x, 2)).join(', ')}</td><td>{ms(c.added_ms, 2)}</td><td>{c.live_each_ms ? c.live_each_ms.map((x) => ms(x)).join(', ') : 'none'}</td><td>{ms(c.http_ms)}</td></tr>;
        })}
      </table>
    </>
  ))}

  <p class="pagelinks"><a href="/learn/caching/">Caching and replay</a> · <a href="/install/">Install</a> · <a href="/functions/">Functions</a></p>
</Base>
